                Each <code>storybook-&lt;fw&gt;</code> server emits and serves
                its own <code>components.json</code> — Angular's from
                <code>angular-component-meta</code>, Vue's from
                <code>vue-component-meta</code>, React's from
                <code>react-docgen</code> (ADR-0097). Names, variants and
                defaults come back shaped for the framework you asked: two-way
                <code>[(checked)]</code> bindings and split Inputs/Outputs for
                Angular, <code>v-model</code>/<code>update:*</code> events and
                typed slots for Vue. The manifest settles a prop name or axis
                value you're unsure of; inside the atelier monorepo,
                <code>libs/spec/src/index.ts</code> stays the cross-framework
                join key the drift gates read, and the component's own
                micro-contract only names the master and any intentional
                Figma↔code mismatches. Neither settles binding shape (two-way
                vs. one-way, slot vs. prop, an event's payload), which the
                format doesn't express; for that, the framework's own manifest
                is the answer.
